{"name":"Touch of Blush","hex":"#f6ded5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"touch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Primarily physical senses.\n To make physical contact with; to bring the hand, finger or other part of the body into contact with. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","To make physical contact with; to bring the hand, finger or other part of the body into contact with. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of touching, especially with the hand or finger.","The faculty or sense of perception by physical contact."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/touch"},{"term":"blush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of blushing; a pink or red glow on the face caused by embarrassment, shame, shyness, love, etc.","A glow; a flush of colour, especially pink or red."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become red or pink in the face (and sometimes experience an associated feeling of warmth), especially due to shyness, love, shame, excitement, or embarrassment.","To be shy, ashamed, or embarrassed (to do something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Somatosensory system","blocks":[{"text":"The somatosensory system, or somatic sensory system, is a subset of the sensory nervous system. The main functions of the somatosensory system are the perception of external stimuli, the perception of internal stimuli, and the regulation of body position and balance (proprioception). It is believed to act as a pathway between the different sensory modalities within the body."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Somatosensory_System.png/330px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somatosensory_system"},{"title":"Blush (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Blush is a medium bright tone of pink."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Blush_rose_1.jpg/330px-Blush_rose_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blush_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg/500px-Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg/500px-Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Touch is a crucial means of receiving information. This photo shows tactile markings identifying stairs for visually impaired people.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png/500px-Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png/1280px-Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png/500px-Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This diagram linearly (unless otherwise mentioned) tracks the projections of all known structures that allow for touch to their relevant endpoints in the human brain.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Gray759.png/330px-Gray759.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Gray759.png/330px-Gray759.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gray's Anatomy, figure 759: the sensory tract, showing the pathway (blue) up the spinal cord, through the somatosensory thalamus, to S1 (Brodmann areas 3, 1, and 2), S2, and BA7","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray759.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Gray717.png/330px-Gray717.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Gray717.png/330px-Gray717.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gray's Anatomy, figure 717: detail showing path adjacent to the insular cortex (marked insula in this figure), adjacent to S1, S2, and BA7","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray717.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Tickle.jpg/500px-Tickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Tickle.jpg/1280px-Tickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Tickle.jpg/500px-Tickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Touch can result in many different physiological reactions such as laughing at being tickled.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATickle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik